# record.reconciled: 96-byte header then a 16-byte body (RFC 28 section 2.3:
# "zero", "0, 0", "zero"). It names no file, no version and no share tag; the
# body is the range it marks counted — the segment the range lies in, then its
# start and end in it. A body of all zeros would settle every reconcile
# record with a lower sequence number.
44 4a 52 31                 # [0,4)   magic "DJR1"
10                          # [4]     kind 16, reconciled
00 00 00                    # [5,8)   reserved
01 02 03 04 05 06 07 08     # [8,16)  segment id 0x0807060504030201
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     # [16,32) FileID zero: a reconciled names no file
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     # [32,40) file offset 0, as the kinds table has it
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     # [40,48) length 0, as the kinds table has it
50 51 52 53 54 55 56 57     # [48,56) sequence number 0x5756555453525150
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     # [56,64) version epoch, always zero in this format
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00     # [64,72) version counter zero: a reconciled names no version
00 00 00 00                 # [72,76) share tag 0: the journal's own, no share's
48 49 4a 4b 4c 4d 4e 4f     # [76,84) synced-through offset 0x4f4e4d4c4b4a4948
61 ac 07 36                 # [84,88) payload CRC32C of identity then the 16 body
                            #         bytes, 0x3607ac61
d8 d9 da db                 # [88,92) own offset in its segment 0xdbdad9d8
84 08 19 2c                 # [92,96) header CRC32C of identity then [0,92), 0x2c190884
82 83 84 85 86 87 88 89     # [96,104) the counted range's segment 0x8988878685848382
8a 8b 8c 8d                 # [104,108) the range's start in it 0x8d8c8b8a
8e 8f 90 91                 # [108,112) the range's end in it 0x91908f8e
